The provided code is an implementation of ArrayList class in C#, which provides a resizable list.

Based on the comment "// @@@ If the return value is true, first index of x in the array is more than or same as 0", we can infer that the code's purpose of this verification is to check whether the first index of `value` in the list is greater than or equal to 0.

To replicate this as an Assert statement, we need to access the return value of IndexOf method before comparing it with 0. The Assert statement will be:

<code>
Assert.IsTrue((_size) == Array.IndexOf((Array)_items, (Object)value, 0, _size));
</code>

Note that, we use the Assert statement instead of Debug.Assert because it will throw an AssertFailedException if the condition is false.

This assert statement checks if the returned index from the ArrayList's IndexOf method is equal to the current size of the ArrayList before the line it is made. This is an extra precaution to ensure that the size of the ArrayList is equal to or greater than the index returned by IndexOf for the condition to hold true.--------------------Based on the given code, the assert statement should be:

